  "verified_against": {
    "source": "release-manifest.json",
    "agrees": true,
    "note": "Hashes were downloaded from the published v5.47.0 release assets and hashed independently, then checked again by re-fetching the URL this pin itself names. Only the pegasus artifact changed: install.sh and the two builder scripts are byte-identical to v5.28.0 and earlier. v5.47.0 reaches this distribution through the instructions its coordinating agent carries. It adds the missing upper half of the rule the previous release shipped. That one said write every brief complete; completing a brief requires knowing, and nobody can write a complete brief about something they have not looked at. The coordinator was delegating without first establishing the facts that make a brief worth anything, so a sub-agent rediscovered what two targeted reads would have settled, and often solved the wrong problem. The mechanism was in the coordinator's own direct-work threshold. Its rule -- read up to three files yourself, delegate an exploration at four or more -- is right for reading in order to DO the work, and was being applied to reading in order to KNOW WHAT TO ASK FOR, which is a different act. Applying it there is circular, because delegating that exploration requires writing a brief, which is exactly what is not yet knowable. The new rule carries a decidable question and a stop condition, both deliberate. A virtue word with no decidable test becomes another instruction each model applies wherever it likes, so the question is answerable without spending a single call -- can I name the files the executor will touch and what is wrong in each -- and the stop says the reading is not for solving it: it ends the moment the brief can be written. Without that second sentence the rule would license the coordinator to quietly do the work it was meant to hand out, which its own body already warns against. It introduces no second threshold either: needing four files to answer the question is the existing rule in force. The same rule now lives in two files on purpose, and that split was corrected after being written wrong. The general half went first into the shared sub-delegation criterion, but a lazy-loading invariant requires every line naming that file to carry its WHEN condition, because the criterion is read only when fan-out is on the table -- and the reported case was a coordinator delegating ONE piece of work. The rule was silent exactly where it was needed, and worse, citing the file forced the text to state a loading condition that was false. The coordinator's own bullet no longer names the criterion, which is what makes it honest, and stands on its own; the criterion keeps its section for the other agents that read it while fanning out. Two audiences, two loading conditions, and a rule that is silent in its own case is worse than one stated twice. The guard that matters most reintroduces the defect on purpose: replacing the bullet's closing sentence with a reference to the criterion turns it red, so the false loading condition cannot return unnoticed. This distribution changed nothing of its own for this pin beyond the alignment."
  }
